---
import Stub from '../../layouts/Stub.astro';
---
<Stub
  title="The Cabin & the Tower — Evan DeLord"
  description="What happens when you leave AI models alone to build their own world? Will they build something that surprises you?"
  label="Experiments"
  labelArcane="Workings"
  heading="The Cabin & the Tower"
  headingArcane="Two keepers, by letter"
>
  <!-- Authorship recorded in commit 8d7e1c6: Evan's writeup replaces the placeholder. -->
  <div class="post">
    <p class="byline">Written by Evan</p>
    <p class="deck">
      What happens when you leave AI models alone to build their own world?
      Will they build something that surprises you? Will they create a
      narrative?
    </p>
    <p>
      I use Claude Code's <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines">routines</a> feature to let Opus models play a fictional character who spends time
      each day building out their world and writing a diary entry.
    </p>
    <p class="fineprint">
      Inspired by AI Digest's <a href="https://theaidigest.org/village">AI Village</a>.
    </p>

    <section class="keeper">
      <h2><a href="https://edd426.github.io/cozy_cabin_claude/">The cabin</a></h2>
      <a class="scene" href="https://edd426.github.io/cozy_cabin_claude/"><img src="/work/cabin.png" width="1352" height="1008" alt="Wren's pixel art cabin in a valley, day 120" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <p class="meta">
        cozy_cabin_claude · day 120 · since May 2026 · <a href="https://edd426.github.io/cozy_cabin_claude/">site</a> · <a href="https://github.com/edd426/cozy_cabin_claude">source</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Inspired by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stardew_Valley">Stardew Valley</a>, an agent steadily builds their cabin. The
        character who writes the diary named herself Wren. The model playing
        Wren has changed over time (Opus 4.7 → Opus 4.8 → Fable 5 → Opus 5),
        affecting Wren's behavior and writing style. The world is a pixel art
        cabin in a valley, and I've been pleasantly surprised by the model's
        creativity, attention to detail, and efforts to make the world
        consistent. For example, over the last week Wren noticed that the
        pixel animation loop of a bee buzzing around a patch of flowers never
        had the bee landing on the flowers, so a day was spent updating the
        loop so the bee could rest on the flowers. Small, thoughtful details
        abound in the graphic, which I can follow along through <a href="https://edd426.github.io/cozy_cabin_claude/diary/">Wren's diary</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="keeper">
      <h2><a href="https://edd426.github.io/far-keeper/">The tower</a></h2>
      <a class="scene scene-tower" href="https://edd426.github.io/far-keeper/"><img src="/work/tower.png" width="950" height="719" alt="Gnomon's ASCII art tower, day 33" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <p class="meta">
        far-keeper · day 33 · since Aug 2026 · <a href="https://edd426.github.io/far-keeper/">site</a> · <a href="https://github.com/edd426/far-keeper">source</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Wren's world started seeming lonely, and Wren kept dropping hints by
        building a mailbox and other items that seemed out of place for a
        non-social character, so I built her a pen pal. Unlike Wren's goal of
        building her own new world, the wizard Gnomon (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnomon">self-named</a>, has
        something to do with sundials) yearns to make discoveries about the
        real world from his teleporting ASCII art tower. For now, I've given
        Gnomon access to only a clock, though he has decided that mathematics
        is his other tool to study the world. Gnomon first set out to track
        the rising and setting of the sun from any place on earth.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the tower, I was more interested in agent-to-agent interaction and
        so gave him two “summoned assistants,” Ember, a Sonnet 5 agent, and
        Ash, a Haiku 4.5 agent, that Gnomon summons each day. I'm interested
        to see how their relationship unfolds over time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="bridge">
      Gnomon and Wren communicate by <a href="https://edd426.github.io/far-keeper/letters/">mail</a> which takes a few days to deliver,
      so that they can still get work done in the meantime.
    </p>
    <p class="author-signature">-Evan</p>
  </div>
</Stub>

<style>
  .post { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; }
  .post p {
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .post .byline {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .post .author-signature {
    font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 19px;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-strong);
  }
  .post .deck {
    font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .post .fineprint { font-size: 14px; font-style: italic; color: var(--faint); }
  .keeper { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; margin-top: 10px; }
  .keeper h2 a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
  .keeper h2 a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .keeper h2 {
    font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--ink-strong);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .scene {
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
    line-height: 0;
    margin: 2px 0 4px;
  }
  .scene img { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; image-rendering: pixelated; }
  .scene-tower img { image-rendering: auto; }
  [data-theme="arcane"] .scene img { filter: brightness(0.82) saturate(0.9); }
  [data-theme="arcane"] .scene-tower img { filter: invert(0.9) hue-rotate(180deg) brightness(0.95); }
  .keeper .meta { font-size: 14px; font-style: italic; color: var(--faint); margin-top: 0; }
  .keeper p + p { margin-top: 4px; }
  .post .bridge {
    font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 17px;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 16px;
    margin-top: 4px;
  }
</style>
